  "narrative": "The differential metabolomics profile reveals a pronounced perturbation in amino acid metabolic networks. RaMP enrichment analysis identifies **Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism (KEGG map00260)** as the top candidate pathway with an extraordinary fold-enrichment of 568.85 (FDR = 0.0), driven by six matched metabolites: ornithine (C00077), 2-ketobutyric acid (C00109), lipoic acid (C16241), S-adenosylhomocysteine (C00021), L-cysteine (C00097), and L-methionine (C00073). A secondary highly enriched term is **Metabolism of amino acids and derivatives (Reactome R-HSA-71291)**, spanning ornithine, 1-pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid, S-adenosylhomocysteine, L-cysteine, L-methionine, and 5,6-dihydroxyindole (FDR = 0.0).\n\n**Key drivers and pathway membership.** S-Adenosylhomocysteine (C00021) is annotated in *Arginine and proline metabolism (KEGG map00330)*, which connects it directly to ornithine and 1-pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid, confirming its centrality to both arginine and polyamine-related branches of this network. Ornithine (C00077) is similarly mapped to polyamine metabolism pathways via Reactome, reinforcing its regulatory role. L-Methionine (C00073) participates in sulfur amino acid metabolism, placing it upstream of the methyl-donor cycle.\n\n**Verified upstream/downstream relationships.** The KEGG reaction graph confirms that L-Methionine (C00073) converts to S-Adenosylhomocysteine (C00021) through the intermediate S-Adenosylmethionine (SAM, C00019) in two enzymatic steps—a canonical methyl-group donation cascade. A second verified path connects Ornithine (C00077) to 1-Pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid (C03912) via glutamate semialdehyde (C01165) and arginine (C00025), establishing ornithine as a precursor to the proline-cycle intermediate.\n\n**Biological significance.** The convergent elevation of methionine-cycle and polyamine-branch intermediates suggests altered one-carbon metabolism and methyl donor availability. S-Adenosylhomocysteine accumulation is particularly notable as a potent inhibitor of methyltransferases, implying widespread epigenetic or post-translational regulation may be suppressed. Ornithine shunting toward 1-pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid hints at proline biosynthesis activation, potentially supporting collagen synthesis or mitochondrial stress adaptation. Meanwhile, nitrate (C00244) alterations may reflect disrupted nitric oxide synthase flux, intersecting with the arginine–ornithine axis. Lipoic acid (C16241) involvement suggests oxidative stress or mitochondrial redox rebalancing, as this cofactor is essential for dehydrogenase complexes. Taken together, the affected metabolites point to a coordinated reprogramming of one-carbon metabolism, amino acid catabolism, and mitochondrial bioenergetics under the treatment condition.",
